You might be interested in this development: holy shit, academic philosophers are finally taking large language models seriously. David Chalmers directly cites @repligate!

@tenobrus: "Holy shit, academic philosophers are finally taking LLMs seriously. David Chalmers directly cites @repligate!"

The linked article discusses how as dialogue agents become increasingly human-like, we need effective ways to describe their behavior without anthropomorphism, foregrounding the concept of role play.

I appreciate @repligate's careful wording here. People often seem to have something different in mind when they talk about LLM role play than what was meant in the Nature paper.

@repligate: "On the difference between (what people mean when they say) 'roleplaying' and 'enactment', and why I think posttrained LLMs especially, but even base models, probably are nontrivially doing the latter, citing some recent empirical evidence to support this."

The linked Nature paper discusses how as dialogue agents become increasingly human-like, we need effective ways to describe their behavior without anthropomorphizing them, foregrounding the concept of role play to utilize familiar psychological terms.
